s":[{"headline":"Hinckley and Bosworth revenues service reports underspend as staff vacancies persist","lead":"The Leicestershire Partnership Revenues & Benefits Joint Committee heard that the shared service handling council tax and benefits had underspent its budget by nearly \u00a394,000 in the four months to the end of July. The shortfall stems mainly from unfilled posts: salaries ran \u00a380,000 below budget, though \u00a329,000 of that reflects a pay award not yet finalised. Officers expect a sizable underspend for the year unless the vacancies are filled soon. The partnership holds reserves, money set aside, of \u00a3765,748, which would rise to \u00a3816,748 if the underspend materialises. A 3.2 per cent pay award is estimated but not yet agreed, creating uncertainty around the final figures.","iso":"2026-09-24","paper_title":"Finance Report","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45515/45515.pdf","byline":"PWLBintel \u00b7 from committee papers","draft":true}],"findings":[{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Leicestershire Partnership Revenues & Benefits Joint Committee","paper_title":"Finance Report","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45515/45515.pdf","headline":"Reserves held at \u00a3765,748 as at 31 March 2026, forecast to reach \u00a3816,748 if underspend materialises.","detail":"Total reserves of \u00a3765,748 comprise \u00a3715,748 earmarked for specific projects (automation review, ICT, IT investment) and \u00a350,000 general reserves. If the \u00a351,000 vacancy-driven underspend is realised at year-end, reserves would increase to \u00a3816,748, strengthening the Partnership's financial position.","category":"reserves","matters":"Growing reserves indicate financial control and resilience; for the council's finance director, this matters because it is their own revenues and benefits service; for a lender, adequate reserves signal ability to manage pressures.","figures":"\u00a3765,748 total reserves at 31 March 2026; \u00a3715,748 earmarked; \u00a350,000 general; forecast \u00a3816,748 if underspend realised","quote":"There are total reserves held as at the 31 March 2026 of \u00a3765,748.","material":true},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Leicestershire Partnership Revenues & Benefits Joint Committee","paper_title":"Finance Report","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45515/45515.pdf","headline":"Pay award estimated at 3.2% but not yet agreed, creating \u00a329,000 forecast uncertainty.","detail":"The Partnership has allocated \u00a329,000 for an estimated 3.2% pay award which has not yet been finalised. The paper explicitly states this allocation 'may be higher if the final agreement is more than this estimate', meaning if the actual pay award exceeds the budgeted rate, the year-end underspend forecast will reduce accordingly.","category":"people","matters":"Forecast precision matters to lenders and finance directors planning for year-end position and reserves management; a pay award higher than budgeted could eliminate or reverse the projected underspend.","figures":"\u00a329,000 allocated for pay award at 3.2% estimated rate","quote":"which may be higher if the final agreement is more than this estimate","material":false},{"iso":"2026-09-24","committee":"Leicestershire Partnership Revenues & Benefits Joint Committee","paper_title":"Finance Report","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45515/45515.pdf","headline":"Revenues & Benefits Partnership reports \u00a393,942 net underspend to July, with \u00a351,000 from staff vacancies expected to persist.","detail":"As at 31 July 2026, the Partnership had a net underspend of \u00a393,942. Salaries underspent by \u00a380,000 due to vacant posts; after accounting for \u00a329,000 budgeted pay award not yet allocated, the true vacancy underspend is \u00a351,000. The paper states vacancies will result in a sizable underspend unless they are filled soon, indicating these absences are unlikely to reverse during the year.","category":"people","matters":"The revenues and benefits service is core to the council's income generation; persistent staff vacancies represent a service delivery risk that a lender or finance director must understand, and the underspend appears structural rather than temporary.","figures":"\u00a393,942 net underspend to 31 July 2026; \u00a380,000 salary underspend; \u00a351,000 vacancy-driven underspend; \u00a329,000 unallocated pay award","quote":"there will be a sizable underspend unless these vacancies are filled soon","material":true},{"iso":"2026-08-24","committee":"Finance & Performance Scrutiny","paper_title":"Treasury management Q1 2026-27 report ib","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45324/45324.pdf","headline":"Council formally identifies insufficient resources to set balanced budget as significant red risk","detail":"In its risk register, the council has flagged as a Net Red (significant) risk that 'the Council has insufficient resources to meet its aspirations and cannot set a balanced budget.' Mitigating actions centre on budget scrutiny and maintaining reserves, suggesting the council is managing structural budget constraints.","category":"governance","matters":"A formally identified red risk of budget insufficiency signals the council acknowledges potential structural funding gaps. This is material to credit assessment as it suggests the council may be approaching or at the edge of its financial capacity.","figures":"No quantified figures; identified as formal Net Red risk in governance risk register","quote":"That the Council has insufficient resources to meet its aspirations and cannot set a balanced budget","material":true},{"iso":"2026-08-24","committee":"Finance & Performance Scrutiny","paper_title":"Treasury management Q1 2026-27 report ib","paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45324/45324.pdf","headline":"Council launching property acquisition programme: \u00a35m long-term fixed-rate borrowing for up to 20 purchases","detail":"The council is borrowing \u00a35m over 25-year terms (rates 5.54%-5.86%, maturing 2051) to purchase general fund properties. As of 30 June 2026, \u00a34m had been advanced to acquire 18 properties at total cost of \u00a34,058,318. This represents a strategic expansion of the council's balance sheet into property income dependency.","category":"capital","matters":"Long-term fixed-rate property acquisitions require rental income to justify the fixed 25-year debt service. Lenders need clarity on projected yields and how downturns in property values or rental income would affect debt coverage, particularly given the elevated rates locked in.","figures":"\u00a35m total; \u00a34m borrowed to date; 18 properties purchased; total cost \u00a34,058,318; rates 5.54%-5.86% maturing 2051","quote":"An additional \u00a35,000,000 in total will be borrowed from PWLB to fund the purchase of up to 20 General Fund properties. As of 30 June 2026 \u00a34,000,000 of this has been borrowed","material":true}],"editorial":null,"freshness":{"last_paper_iso":"2026-09-24"},"name":"Hinckley & Bosworth","daily_post":{"iso":"2026-09-25","since_iso":"2026-08-26","distress":false,"headline":"Reserves held at \u00a3765,748 as at 31 March 2026, forecast to reach \u00a3816,748 if underspend materialises.","lead":"Growing reserves indicate financial control and resilience; for the council's finance director, this matters because it is their own revenues and benefits service; for a lender, adequate reserves signal ability to manage pressures.","also":["2 other findings in this window"],"watch":"Scrutiny Commission on 2026-10-22","n_findings":3,"n_documents":0,"top_paper_title":"Finance Report","top_paper_url":"https://moderngov.hinckley-bosworth.gov.uk/documents/s45515/45515.pdf","draft":true},"findings_total":68,"news_drafts_total":1}}